add('protoceratops andrewsi|Protoceratops andrewsi|First horned face|Granger & Gregory, 1923|Ornithischia|Marginocephalia|Protoceratopsidae|cretaceous|Late Cretaceous|Campanian|75|71|≈1.8–2.5 m|≈100–200 kg|Mongolia|Asia|Djadochta Formation, Gobi Desert|Arid sand dune field|psittacosaurus,triceratops,centrosaurus|A small early ceratopsian with a modest frill and no large facial horns. It is known from abundant material, including nests and eggs.|The skull has a small rostral bone and a short frill; the body is low and the hindlimbs are longer than the forelimbs.|Many skeletons, skulls, nests and eggs from the Gobi Desert.');
add('psittacosaurus mongoliensis|Psittacosaurus mongoliensis|Parrot lizard|Osborn, 1923|Ornithischia|Marginocephalia|Psittacosauridae|cretaceous|Early Cretaceous|Aptian–Albian|126|101|≈1.5–2 m|≈20–40 kg|Mongolia|Asia|Yixian and Jehol groups|Forest and lake margin|protoceratops,triceratops,pachycephalosaurus|A small early ceratopsian with a parrot-like beak and a relatively large head. Some specimens preserve a tail fringe and integument.|The skull is deep and short, with a strong rostral beak; the tail bears a row of tall, filament-like structures in some specimens.|Hundreds of skeletons, including juveniles and soft-tissue impressions.');
add('pachyrhinosaurus canadensis|Pachyrhinosaurus canadensis|Thick-nosed lizard|Sternberg, 1950|Ornithischia|Marginocephalia|Ceratopsidae|cretaceous|Late Cretaceous|Campanian|74|72|≈6–8 m|≈3–4 t|Canada|North America|Horseshoe Canyon Formation, Alberta|Coastal plain|centrosaurus,styracosaurus,einiosaurus|A centrosaurine ceratopsian with a massive bony boss over the nose instead of a tall horn. The boss is well preserved; its function is debated.|The nasal region forms a thick, rugose boss, the frill is moderately long, and the skull is large.|Several skulls and partial skeletons, including bonebed material.');
add('nasutoceratops titusi|Nasutoceratops titusi|Large-nosed horned face|Sampson et al., 2013|Ornithischia|Marginocephalia|Ceratopsidae|cretaceous|Late Cretaceous|Campanian|76|75|≈4.5–5 m|≈1.5–2 t|United States|North America|Kaiparowits Formation, Utah|Coastal plain|centrosaurus,styracosaurus,kosmoceratops|A centrosaurine with a large, hollow nasal boss and forward-curving brow horns. The skull is known; the postcranium is less complete.|The nasal region is expanded into a large boss, and the brow horns are long and curved forward.|A partial skull and associated postcranial material.');
add('kosmoceratops richardsoni|Kosmoceratops richardsoni|Ornamented horned face|Sampson et al., 2010|Ornithischia|Marginocephalia|Ceratopsidae|cretaceous|Late Cretaceous|Campanian|76|75|≈4.5–5 m|≈1.5–2 t|United States|North America|Kaiparowits Formation, Utah|Coastal plain|chasmosaurus,pentaceratops,nasutoceratops|A chasmosaurine with an extremely ornate frill bearing many small hooks and spikes. The frill is real; its display role is inferred.|The frill is broad and short, with multiple epoccipitals, and the brow horns are long and curved.|A skull and partial skeleton from southern Utah.');
add('pentaceratops sternbergii|Pentaceratops sternbergii|Five-horned face|Osborn, 1923|Ornithischia|Marginocephalia|Ceratopsidae|cretaceous|Late Cretaceous|Campanian|76|73|≈6–7 m|≈3–4 t|United States|North America|Kirtland Formation, New Mexico|Coastal plain|chasmosaurus,triceratops,kosmoceratops|A large chasmosaurine with a long frill and three facial horns; the two additional epijugal horns give the genus its name. The frill is thin and highly fenestrated.|The frill is long and broad, the brow horns are large, and the snout is deep.|Several skulls and partial skeletons from the San Juan Basin.');
add('einiosaurus procurvicornis|Einiosaurus procurvicornis|Buffalo lizard|Sampson, 1995|Ornithischia|Marginocephalia|Ceratopsidae|cretaceous|Late Cretaceous|Campanian|75|74|≈4.5–5.5 m|≈1.5–2.5 t|United States|North America|Two Medicine Formation, Montana|Coastal plain|centrosaurus,styracosaurus,pachyrhinosaurus|A centrosaurine with a nasal horn that curves forward and a relatively simple frill. Bonebeds contain several individuals of different ages.|The nasal horn is procumbent, the frill is moderately short, and the postcranial skeleton is robust.|Several bonebeds with skulls and partial skeletons.');
add('chasmosaurus belli|Chasmosaurus belli|Opening lizard|Lambe, 1914|Ornithischia|Marginocephalia|Ceratopsidae|cretaceous|Late Cretaceous|Campanian|77|75|≈4.5–5.5 m|≈2–3 t|Canada|North America|Dinosaur Park Formation, Alberta|Coastal plain|pentaceratops,kosmoceratops,triceratops|A chasmosaurine with a long, fenestrated frill and relatively short brow horns. The frill is thin bone; its display function is inferred.|The frill is large and open, with a scalloped margin, and the brow horns are short.|Several skulls and partial skeletons from Alberta.');

// D. Hadrosaurs and other ornithopods
add('edmontosaurus regalis|Edmontosaurus regalis|Edmonton lizard|Lambe, 1917|Ornithischia|Ornithopoda|Hadrosauridae|cretaceous|Late Cretaceous|Maastrichtian|68|66|≈11–13 m|≈4–6 t|Canada|North America|Horseshoe Canyon Formation, Alberta|Coastal plain|parasaurolophus,corythosaurus,gryposaurus|A large hadrosaurid with a long, low skull and a dental battery. Some specimens preserve a soft-tissue crest on the head, showing that the bony skull is not the whole animal.|The skull is long and flat, the snout is broad, and the jaws carry hundreds of tightly packed teeth.|Many skeletons, including articulated individuals and skin impressions.');
add('corythosaurus casuarius|Corythosaurus casuarius|Helmet lizard|Brown, 1914|Ornithischia|Ornithopoda|Hadrosauridae|cretaceous|Late Cretaceous|Campanian|77|75|≈8–9 m|≈3–4 t|Canada|North America|Dinosaur Park Formation, Alberta|Coastal plain|lambeosaurus,parasaurolophus,edmontosaurus|A hadrosaurid with a tall, helmet-like crest formed by the nasal and frontal bones. The crest is hollow and connects to the nasal passages.|The skull has a high domed crest, a broad beak, and a large dental battery.|Several complete skulls and articulated skeletons, some with skin impressions.');
add('lambeosaurus lambei|Lambeosaurus lambei|Lambe’s lizard|Parks, 1923|Ornithischia|Ornithopoda|Hadrosauridae|cretaceous|Late Cretaceous|Campanian|77|75|≈9–10 m|≈3–4 t|Canada|North America|Dinosaur Park Formation, Alberta|Coastal plain|corythosaurus,parasaurolophus,edmontosaurus|A hadrosaurid with a hatchet-shaped crest and a hollow nasal passage. The crest shape varies between species and growth stages.|The crest is tall and hatchet-shaped, and the skull is long with a broad, toothless beak.|Several skulls and partial skeletons from Alberta.');
add('maiasaura peeblesorum|Maiasaura peeblesorum|Good mother lizard|Horner & Makela, 1979|Ornithischia|Ornithopoda|Hadrosauridae|cretaceous|Late Cretaceous|Campanian|77|75|≈7–8 m|≈2–3 t|United States|North America|Two Medicine Formation, Montana|Coastal plain|edmontosaurus,gryposaurus,protoceratops|A hadrosaurid known from nests, eggs and juveniles as well as adults. The nesting association is strong evidence of colonial nesting, but the extent of parental care is inferred.|The skull is low and broad, with a small crest, and the dental battery is well developed.|An exceptional growth series, nests and eggs from Montana.');
add('gryposaurus notabilis|Gryposaurus notabilis|Hooked-nose lizard|Lambe, 1914|Ornithischia|Ornithopoda|Hadrosauridae|cretaceous|Late Cretaceous|Campanian|77|75|≈8–9 m|≈3–4 t|Canada|North America|Dinosaur Park Formation, Alberta|Coastal plain|edmontosaurus,corythosaurus,maiasaura|A hadrosaurid with a prominent arched nasal crest. The crest is a bony structure; its soft-tissue covering is not preserved.|The nasal region is strongly arched, the skull is large, and the dental battery is extensive.|Several skulls and partial skeletons from Alberta and Montana.');
add('ouranosaurus nigeriensis|Ouranosaurus nigeriensis|Brave monitor|Taquet, 1976|Ornithischia|Ornithopoda|Iguanodontidae|cretaceous|Early Cretaceous|Aptian–Albian|125|112|≈7–8 m|≈2–3 t|Niger|Africa|Elrhaz Formation, Gadoufaoua|Riverine floodplain|iguanodon,iguanacolossus,muttaburrasaurus|An iguanodontian with elongated neural spines along the back. The spines supported a ridge or sail-like structure; the soft tissue is not preserved.|The dorsal neural spines are tall, the forelimbs are robust, and the hand bears a thumb spike.|Several partial skeletons, including a relatively complete skull and postcrania.');
add('tenontosaurus tilletti|Tenontosaurus tilletti|Sinew lizard|Ostrom, 1970|Ornithischia|Ornithopoda|Tenontosauridae|cretaceous|Early Cretaceous|Aptian–Albian|115|108|≈6–7 m|≈1–2 t|United States|North America|Cloverly Formation, Montana and Wyoming|Coastal plain|iguanodon,dryosaurus,camptosaurus|A medium-sized iguanodontian with a long tail and a low, deep body. It is often found with Deinonychus remains, but the association does not prove a predator-prey encounter.|The tail is long and deep, the forelimbs are moderately long, and the skull is small relative to the body.|Many partial skeletons, including juveniles and associated Deinonychus material.');
add('dryosaurus altus|Dryosaurus altus|Oak lizard|Marsh, 1878|Ornithischia|Ornithopoda|Dryosauridae|jurassic|Late Jurassic|Kimmeridgian–Tithonian|155|145|≈3–4 m|≈100–200 kg|United States|North America|Morrison Formation, Colorado and Utah|Floodplain woodland|hypsilophodon,camptosaurus,tenontosaurus|A small, agile ornithopod with long hindlimbs and a short, toothless beak. It is known from partial skeletons and abundant isolated material.|The hindlimbs are long, the forelimbs are short, and the skull is small with a beak and cheek teeth.|Several partial skeletons and skulls from the Morrison Formation.');
add('camptosaurus dispar|Camptosaurus dispar|Flexible lizard|Marsh, 1879|Ornithischia|Ornithopoda|Camptosauridae|jurassic|Late Jurassic|Kimmeridgian–Tithonian|155|148|≈6–7 m|≈1–2 t|United States|North America|Morrison Formation, Wyoming and Utah|Floodplain woodland|dryosaurus,iguanodon,tenontosaurus|A medium-sized ornithopod with a robust body and a thumb spike. It is one of the more completely known Morrison ornithopods.|The skull is relatively small, the forelimbs are shorter than the hindlimbs, and the hand bears a small thumb spike.|Several partial skeletons and skulls, including articulated material.');
add('hypsilophodon foxii|Hypsilophodon foxii|High-ridged tooth|Huxley, 1869|Ornithischia|Ornithopoda|Hypsilophodontidae|cretaceous|Early Cretaceous|Barremian–Aptian|130|125|≈1.8–2.3 m|≈20–50 kg|United Kingdom|Europe|Wessex Formation, Isle of Wight|Coastal floodplain|dryosaurus,orodromeus,leaellynasaura|A small ornithopod with a lightweight skull and long hindlimbs. Early reconstructions placed it in trees; the anatomy supports a primarily terrestrial animal.|The skull is small with a beak and cheek teeth, the hindlimbs are long, and the tail is stiffened by ossified tendons.|Many partial skeletons from the Isle of Wight.');
add('orodromeus makelai|Orodromeus makelai|Mountain runner|Horner & Weishampel, 1988|Ornithischia|Ornithopoda|Thescelosauridae|cretaceous|Late Cretaceous|Campanian|77|75|≈2–2.5 m|≈20–40 kg|United States|North America|Two Medicine Formation, Montana|Coastal plain|hypsilophodon,leaellynasaura,dryosaurus|A small ornithopod known from skeletons and eggs. The eggs first attributed to Orodromeus may belong to another dinosaur, so the nesting evidence is not secure.|The skeleton is lightly built, with long hindlimbs and a small skull.|Several skeletons and egg clutches from Montana.');
add('leaellynasaura amicagraphica|Leaellynasaura amicagraphica|Leaellyn’s lizard|Rich & Rich, 1989|Ornithischia|Ornithopoda|Ornithopoda incertae sedis|cretaceous|Early Cretaceous|Aptian–Albian|118|110|≈1–1.5 m|≈3–5 kg|Australia|Australia|Eumeralla Formation, Victoria|Polar woodland|hypsilophodon,orodromeus,muttaburrasaurus|A small ornithopod from a high-latitude Australian environment. It lived within the polar circle and experienced long seasonal darkness.|The skeleton is lightly built, with long hindlimbs and a long tail; the skull is incompletely known.|Several partial skeletons from the Eumeralla Formation.');
add('muttaburrasaurus langdoni|Muttaburrasaurus langdoni|Muttaburra lizard|Bartholomai & Molnar, 1981|Ornithischia|Ornithopoda|Iguanodontidae|cretaceous|Early Cretaceous|Albian|107|103|≈7–8 m|≈2–3 t|Australia|Australia|Mackunda Formation, Queensland|Floodplain woodland|iguanodon,ouranosaurus,leaellynasaura|A large iguanodontian with a deep snout and a bulbous nasal region. Its diet is inferred as herbivorous; the snout may have supported a soft-tissue display structure.|The skull has a deep, rounded snout and a robust dental battery; the postcranial skeleton is bulky.|A partial skeleton and skull from Queensland.');
add('iguanacolossus fortis|Iguanacolossus fortis|Iguanodon colossus|McDonald et al., 2010|Ornithischia|Ornithopoda|Iguanodontidae|cretaceous|Early Cretaceous|Barremian–Aptian|130|124|≈9–10 m|≈3–4 t|United States|North America|Cedar Mountain Formation, Utah|Coastal plain|iguanodon,ouranosaurus,muttaburrasaurus|A large iguanodontian known from a partial skull and postcranial skeleton. Its size is estimated from incomplete material.|The skull is large and deep, and the postcranial skeleton is robust; the hand bears a thumb spike.|A partial skull and associated postcrania from Utah.');
add('rhabdodon priscus|Rhabdodon priscus|Primitive rod tooth|Matheron, 1869|Ornithischia|Ornithopoda|Rhabdodontidae|cretaceous|Late Cretaceous|Maastrichtian|72|69|≈4–6 m|≈250–500 kg|France|Europe|Grès à Reptiles, Provence|Coastal floodplain|iguanodon,tenontosaurus,muttaburrasaurus|A late-surviving iguanodontian from Europe, with a robust body and a deep skull. Its relationships to other iguanodontians are debated.|The skull is relatively deep, the forelimbs are shorter than the hindlimbs, and the dental battery is less specialized than in hadrosaurs.|Several partial skeletons and skulls from southern France and Spain.');

// E. Sauropods and giant long-necked dinosaurs
add('argentinosaurus huinculensis|Argentinosaurus huinculensis|Argentine lizard|Bonaparte & Coria, 1993|Sauropodomorpha|Sauropoda|Titanosauridae|cretaceous|Late Cretaceous|Cenomanian–Turonian|96|92|≈30–35 m|≈60–80 t|Argentina|South America|Huincul Formation, Neuquén|Riverine floodplain|patagotitan,dreadnoughtus,puertasaurus|A giant titanosaur known from partial vertebrae, ribs and limb bones. Its enormous size is estimated from a fragmentary holotype, so the upper range is uncertain.|The preserved dorsal vertebrae are large and highly pneumatised, and the limb bones are exceptionally robust.|A partial postcranial skeleton; no skull or complete femur.');
add('patagotitan mayorum|Patagotitan mayorum|Patagonian titan|Carballido et al., 2017|Sauropodomorpha|Sauropoda|Titanosauridae|cretaceous|Late Cretaceous|Albian–Cenomanian|101|95|≈31–37 m|≈55–70 t|Argentina|South America|Cerro Barcino Formation, Chubut|Riverine floodplain|argentinosaurus,dreadnoughtus,futalognkosaurus|A giant titanosaur known from several associated skeletons from a single quarry. The material is unusually complete for a giant sauropod.|The presacral vertebrae are highly pneumatised, the forelimbs are long, and the femur is massive.|Several associated skeletons, including limb bones and vertebrae.');
add('dreadnoughtus schrani|Dreadnoughtus schrani|Fear nothing|Lacovara et al., 2014|Sauropodomorpha|Sauropoda|Titanosauridae|cretaceous|Late Cretaceous|Campanian–Maastrichtian|76|70|≈22–26 m|≈22–38 t|Argentina|South America|Cerro Fortaleza Formation, Santa Cruz|Riverine floodplain|argentinosaurus,patagotitan,puertasaurus|A large titanosaur known from two partial skeletons with a relatively complete appendicular skeleton. The body mass is estimated from limb proportions.|The femur and humerus are robust, the tail is long, and the pelvis is wide; the skull is represented by a single tooth.|Two partial skeletons from the same locality, including a major part of the appendicular skeleton.');
add('puertasaurus reuili|Puertasaurus reuili|Puerta’s lizard|Novas et al., 2005|Sauropodomorpha|Sauropoda|Titanosauridae|cretaceous|Late Cretaceous|Maastrichtian|72|66|≈30–35 m|≈50–70 t|Argentina|South America|Pari Aike Formation, Santa Cruz|Riverine floodplain|argentinosaurus,patagotitan,dreadnoughtus|A giant titanosaur known from a few vertebrae and ribs. The record is too fragmentary to reconstruct the whole animal confidently.|The dorsal vertebrae are enormous and highly pneumatised; the preserved ribs are very long.|A few vertebrae and ribs; no limb bones or skull.');
add('futalognkosaurus dukei|Futalognkosaurus dukei|Giant chief lizard|Calvo et al., 2007|Sauropodomorpha|Sauropoda|Titanosauridae|cretaceous|Late Cretaceous|Turonian–Coniacian|94|85|≈25–28 m|≈30–45 t|Argentina|South America|Portezuelo Formation, Neuquén|Riverine floodplain|patagotitan,argentinosaurus,dreadnoughtus|A large titanosaur with a relatively complete axial skeleton. It helps document the anatomy of titanosaur necks and pelves.|The neck is long, the dorsal vertebrae are pneumatised, and the pelvis is wide; the skull is unknown.|A partial skeleton including much of the axial column and pelvis.');
add('saltasaurus loricatus|Saltasaurus loricatus|Salta lizard|Bonaparte & Powell, 1980|Sauropodomorpha|Sauropoda|Saltasauridae|cretaceous|Late Cretaceous|Campanian–Maastrichtian|76|70|≈12–15 m|≈7–10 t|Argentina|South America|Lecho Formation, Salta|Riverine floodplain|nigersaurus,patagotitan,dreadnoughtus|A relatively small titanosaur with bony armour plates embedded in the skin. It was among the first sauropods shown to have dermal armour.|The body is covered in small osteoderms, the neck is moderately long, and the limbs are robust.|Several partial skeletons, including armour and eggs.');
add('dicraeosaurus hansemanni|Dicraeosaurus hansemanni|Forked lizard|Janensch, 1914|Sauropodomorpha|Sauropoda|Dicraeosauridae|jurassic|Late Jurassic|Kimmeridgian–Tithonian|152|145|≈12–13 m|≈6–8 t|Tanzania|Africa|Tendaguru Formation, Lindi|Coastal plain|bajadasaurus,diplodocus,apatosaurus|A dicraeosaurid with a relatively short neck and deeply forked neural spines. The spines are tall but do not form the extreme sail of Amargasaurus.|The cervical vertebrae have tall, bifurcated neural spines; the neck is shorter than in diplodocids.|Several partial skeletons from Tendaguru.');
add('nigersaurus taqueti|Nigersaurus taqueti|Niger lizard|Sereno et al., 1999|Sauropodomorpha|Sauropoda|Rebbachisauridae|cretaceous|Early Cretaceous|Aptian–Albian|115|110|≈9–10 m|≈4–6 t|Niger|Africa|Elrhaz Formation, Gadoufaoua|Riverine floodplain|dicraeosaurus,diplodocus,bajadasaurus|A rebbachisaurid with a wide, squared-off snout and a dental battery that replaced teeth rapidly. It likely grazed low vegetation.|The skull is broad and shallow, the jaw carries many small teeth, and the neck is relatively short.|A nearly complete skull and partial skeleton, plus additional material.');
add('shunosaurus lii|Shunosaurus lii|Shu lizard|Dong, Zhou & Zhang, 1983|Sauropodomorpha|Sauropoda|Eusauropoda|jurassic|Middle Jurassic|Bajocian–Callovian|170|160|≈9–10 m|≈3–4 t|China|Asia|Shaximiao Formation, Sichuan|Riverine woodland|mamenchisaurus,camarasaurus,jobaria|An early sauropod with a relatively short neck and a small tail club formed by fused vertebrae. It is known from many individuals.|The neck is shorter than in later sauropods, and the distal tail bears a bony club.|Many partial skeletons, including complete skulls and tail clubs.');
add('mamenchisaurus hochuanensis|Mamenchisaurus hochuanensis|Mamenchi lizard|Young & Zhao, 1972|Sauropodomorpha|Sauropoda|Mamenchisauridae|jurassic|Late Jurassic|Oxfordian|161|155|≈20–22 m|≈15–25 t|China|Asia|Shangshaximiao Formation, Sichuan|Riverine woodland|shunosaurus,camarasaurus,brachiosaurus|A long-necked sauropod with one of the most elongated necks among sauropods. The neck is composed of 19 elongated vertebrae.|The cervical vertebrae are extremely long, the forelimbs are shorter than the hindlimbs, and the tail is long.|Several partial skeletons and skull elements from Sichuan.');
add('camarasaurus supremus|Camarasaurus supremus|Chambered lizard|Cope, 1877|Sauropodomorpha|Sauropoda|Camarasauridae|jurassic|Late Jurassic|Kimmeridgian–Tithonian|155|145|≈15–18 m|≈15–20 t|United States|North America|Morrison Formation, Colorado and Utah|Floodplain woodland|brachiosaurus,diplodocus,apatosaurus|A common Morrison sauropod with a relatively short neck, a deep skull and spoon-shaped teeth. It is known from many individuals and growth stages.|The skull is large and deep, the neck is shorter than in diplodocids, and the limbs are robust.|Many partial and articulated skeletons, including juveniles.');
add('europasaurus holgeri|Europasaurus holgeri|European lizard|Mateus et al., 2006|Sauropodomorpha|Sauropoda|Brachiosauridae|jurassic|Late Jurassic|Kimmeridgian|154|151|≈5–6.5 m|≈1–2 t|Germany|Europe|Süntel Formation, Lower Saxony|Island woodland|brachiosaurus,camarasaurus,mamenchisaurus|A small brachiosaurid from an island environment, interpreted as a case of insular dwarfism. The size reduction is supported by a growth series.|The skeleton is miniature for a brachiosaurid but retains the high shoulders and long forelimbs of the group.|Many individuals, including juveniles and adults, from a single locality.');
add('jobaria tiguidensis|Jobaria tiguidensis|Jobar lizard|Sereno et al., 1999|Sauropodomorpha|Sauropoda|Eusauropoda|jurassic|Middle Jurassic|Bathonian–Oxfordian|167|161|≈18–21 m|≈15–20 t|Niger|Africa|Elrhaz Formation, Gadoufaoua|Riverine floodplain|shunosaurus,camarasaurus,brachiosaurus|A large sauropod with a relatively simple vertebral column and a short neck for its body size. Its relationships among early sauropods are debated.|The neck is short and the tail is long; the forelimbs are robust and the skull is incompletely known.|A partial skeleton and referred material from Niger.');
add('turiasaurus riodevensis|Turiasaurus riodevensis|Turia lizard|Royo-Torres et al., 2006|Sauropodomorpha|Sauropoda|Turiasauria|jurassic|Late Jurassic|Tithonian|150|145|≈30–37 m|≈40–50 t|Spain|Europe|Villar del Arzobispo Formation, Teruel|Coastal floodplain|jobaria,camarasaurus,brachiosaurus|A very large sauropod from Europe, known from a partial skeleton and referred teeth. Its size is estimated from limb bones.|The humerus is massive, the teeth are large and spatulate, and the appendicular skeleton is robust.|A partial skeleton and isolated teeth from Spain.');

// F. Unusual, lesser-known and scientifically interesting dinosaurs
add('deinocheirus mirificus|Deinocheirus mirificus|Terrible hand|Osmólska & Roniewicz, 1970|Theropoda|Maniraptora|Deinocheiridae|cretaceous|Late Cretaceous|Maastrichtian|70|66|≈11 m|≈6–7 t|Mongolia|Asia|Nemegt Formation, Gobi Desert|Riverine floodplain|gallimimus,therizinosaurus,oviraptor|A very large ornithomimosaur with enormous forelimbs, a humped back and a broad, toothless beak. It was known only from arms for decades before complete skeletons were found.|The forelimbs are exceptionally long and robust, the dorsal neural spines are tall, and the skull is elongated and edentulous.|Two nearly complete skeletons and isolated elements from Mongolia.');
add('yutyrannus huali|Yutyrannus huali|Feathered tyrant|Xu et al., 2012|Theropoda|Coelurosauria|Proceratosauridae|cretaceous|Early Cretaceous|Aptian|125|124|≈8–9 m|≈1.4 t|China|Asia|Yixian Formation, Liaoning|Temperate forest and lake margin|dilong,guanlong,tyrannosaurus|A large tyrannosauroid known from three individuals with preserved filamentous integument. It shows that large body size and a feathered covering coexisted.|The skull is deep and the forelimbs are short; filamentous structures are preserved around the body and tail.|Three partial skeletons with extensive integument impressions.');
add('guanlong wucaii|Guanlong wucaii|Five-coloured crown dragon|Xu et al., 2006|Theropoda|Coelurosauria|Proceratosauridae|jurassic|Late Jurassic|Oxfordian|161|158|≈3 m|≈50–75 kg|China|Asia|Shishugou Formation, Xinjiang|Riverine woodland|dilong,yutyrannus,compsognathus|An early tyrannosauroid with a prominent median crest on the skull. The crest is thin bone and likely had a display function.|The skull bears a large, delicate crest formed by the nasals, and the body is lightly built.|Two partial skeletons, one immature and one adult.');
add('dilong paradoxus|Dilong paradoxus|Paradoxical emperor dragon|Xu et al., 2004|Theropoda|Coelurosauria|Proceratosauridae|cretaceous|Early Cretaceous|Barremian–Aptian|126|123|≈1.5–2 m|≈10–20 kg|China|Asia|Yixian Formation, Liaoning|Temperate forest and lake margin|yutyrannus,guanlong,compsognathus|A small early tyrannosauroid with preserved filamentous integument. It provides evidence that feathers were present in the tyrannosauroid lineage.|The skeleton is lightly built, the forelimbs are relatively long, and filamentous structures are preserved around the tail and body.|A partial skeleton with integument impressions.');
